# 34. Threat Model

The framework itself must be protected from adversarial manipulation.

Potential threats include:

* poisoned security data,
* manipulated security memory,
* malicious policy updates,
* compromised AI recommendations,
* false alerts,
* adversarial inputs,
* compromised dependencies,
* unauthorized policy changes,
* rollback abuse,
* privilege escalation.

Therefore, the adaptive security mechanism becomes part of the security boundary.

---

# 35. Security of the Security System

A central principle is:

> **The mechanism responsible for adapting security must itself be secured.**

Controls should include:

* authentication,
* authorization,
* signed policy changes,
* audit logging,
* version control,
* integrity checks,
* protected configuration,
* rollback,
* human approval,
* isolated testing,
* monitoring of the adaptation engine itself.

---

# 36. Failure-Safe Behaviour

When the adaptive mechanism cannot confidently determine an appropriate action, it should fail safely.

Examples include:

```text
Insufficient Evidence
        ↓
No Automatic Adaptation
        ↓
Record Event
        ↓
Request Review
```

Similarly:

```text
Policy Test Failure
        ↓
Reject Candidate
        ↓
Retain Existing Policy
```

This establishes the existing policy as the default safety boundary.

